language is a human universal  every people every culture has developed  its own language  in fact there are over 6 000 languages  in use today and historically there have  been many many more  but written language the written word  was not developed everywhere  independently  while there are thousands of spoken  languages there are only a few hundred  writing systems and only a handful of  those writing systems are widely and or  often used  the most widespread of these systems is  the latin alphabet it's seen throughout  western europe the americas australia  southern africa turkey and more it is  also the base of our own english  alphabet but where did the alphabet come  from how has it changed over time and  why is it so widespread today  [Music]  our story starts with one of the oldest  civilizations in the world  egypt  egypt had a complicated and  sophisticated system of writing that we  now know as hieroglyphics hieroglyphs  were pictograms  small drawings that represent objects  animals and concepts this type of  writing system is called a logographic  system  so how did this complicated system of  over 700 pictograms condense into the  alphabet we know today  well it took a lot of time in around the  16th or 17th century bc egypt had grown  its empire far outside the river nile  and into libya down to ethiopia and most  importantly for our story up into asia  specifically to the land of canaan  the local canaanites would not normally  have been taught the full egyptian  script which was mostly used by priests  and scribes  that being said some canaanites somehow  or another were able to take many of the  hieroglyphs and fit them into their own  language  they took some of the common characters  simplified them and gave them each a  sound the sounds corresponded to the  pictograms based on their meaning for  example the character per which in  hieroglyphics meant house  was given the sound  for the canaanite word for house  baitu this alphabet has now come to be  known as the proto-cynic alphabet named  for the sinai peninsula where many of  the surviving records of the script have  been found  once the egyptian empire receded in the  12th century bc the canaanites or by  this point more commonly called  phoenicians quickly became the most  dominant trading power in the  mediterranean they built an extensive  maritime network from north africa egypt  italy and most importantly for our story  greece  by this point the proto-cynic alphabet  evolved into the phoenician alphabet it  greatly simplified the pictograms used  by the egyptians into almost completely  abstract symbols a few of which you may  recognize the phoenician trading network  transferred many things both material  and immaterial around the mediterranean  one such thing was their alphabet the  phoenician alphabet was copied by many  groups of people the arameans in syria  the hebrews in palestine and the greeks  in greece  according to the greek historian  herodotus the alphabet was brought to  greece by a phoenician prince named  kadmos he was also an important greek  hero of the likes of heracles and  perseus he was certainly a legendary  figure though and not a historical one  as you can see the archaic greek  alphabet is quite similar to the  phoenician one but the letters have  evolved and a few more were added  the most important innovation the greeks  made was the addition of vowels you see  the previous alphabets were not true  alphabets but abjads  abjads are writing systems similar to  alphabets in that each letter represents  one sound but an abjad does not include  vowels  instead the vowels are implied through  context modern abjads include hebrew and  arabic the greeks took letters in the  phoenician alphabet that they had no use  for and substituted them for vowel  sounds for example the letter alep which  represented a glottal stop a sound not  used in greek became alpha which made  the ah sound  the archaic greek alphabet had two main  variants  an eastern variant and a western one the  eastern script was used in athens and it  evolved into the modern greek alphabet  as well as the cyrillic alphabet used in  much of eastern europe  the western greek alphabet was used in  the western greek colonies you see in  the 8th century bc  some of the greek city-states learning  from their phoenician neighbors created  colonies throughout the mediterranean  the eubans who used the western alphabet  created colonies in southern italy and  this is where the greek alphabet was  spread to the etruscans  the etruscans were the dominant italian  civilization before the rise of rome  their alphabet was very similar to the  western greek but they dropped some of  the letters they had no use for  by the 7th century bc the romans had  interacted with the etruscans enough  that they adopted their alphabet over  time it became the latin alphabet that  we know and love  as you can see they added the letters y  and z to the end  this happened around the time alexander  the great was conquering the persian  empire yep the romans were around at the  same time as alexander history overlaps  itself sometimes  the final form of the latin alphabet was  formalized during the roman empire the  roman language latin was spread  throughout western europe eventually  even areas where latin wasn't spoken  like the german lands scandinavia and  the british isles adopted their script  replacing their previous runic alphabets  the anglo-saxons adopted the latin  alphabet for old english around the time  they were christianized  the english alphabet actually has its  own history as you can see the latin  alphabet doesn't include everything we  do  for one it's all capital letters as  romans didn't have a use for lowercase  also the letters j u and w are all  missing this separate history will have  to wait for another time however in the  17th and 18th centuries many of the  european powers started exploring and  eventually colonized much of the world  these new empires spread their languages  and cultures to far-off places in the  americas the most widely spoken  languages are all western european in  africa english and french are very  widely spoken and so on and so forth  with this language spread the latin  alphabet of course spread as well  even many non-european languages now use  the latin alphabet like swahili and east  africa and tagalig in the philippines  even in countries where the latin  alphabet isn't used school children will  often be taught the alphabet because of  how widespread it is  so that's the history of the latin  alphabet in a later video i plan to  explore the ways the english alphabet  evolved after the latin script was  brought to english shores but until then  thank you so much for watching please  leave the video a like share it with a  friend subscribe if you haven't already  and hit the bell icon to be notified  whenever i upload a new video  bye  [Music]
 

